Existing:                                                                                                            


Theoretical Foundations and Architectures                                                                            

Recent research has begun to formalize the mathematical and structural prerequisites for AGI. A notable contribution 
proposes a morphogenetic structure that replaces the traditional subjective unit, defining mental states through     
topological integrity and establishing the mathematical inevitability of AGI [^10.5281_zenodo.19034234].             
Complementing this theoretical work, deep reinforcement learning is explored as a platform-independent framework for 
intelligent robot control, positioning it as a foundational mechanism for AGI applications                           
[^10.48550_arxiv.2105.13806].                                                                                        

New addition for this section:                                                                                       

Recent advancements in cognitive architectures emphasize rule-chaining and hierarchical composition to bridge        
symbolic reasoning and pattern synthesis, achieving high accuracy on abstract reasoning benchmarks [^2609.10654]. To 
address the limitations of opaque parameter-heavy models, probability-wave frameworks incorporating adaptive         
entangled game modules have been proposed to simulate brain-inspired mechanisms for more compact and robust AGI      
systems [^2609.09226]. Furthermore, structured working memory architectures, such as pseudo-video spatial-memory     
Transformers, demonstrate that task-aligned latent workspaces significantly outperform unstructured latent capacity  
in learning world dynamics and sequential prediction [^2609.02339]. Recursive self-improvement mechanisms are also   
being formalized; systems like Meta^n apply recursive operations to their own products to achieve emergent depth     
without destabilizing the core architecture, while Prime Agent provides a harness for long-horizon recursive language
model workflows that standardize execution and recovery [^2608.24735][^2608.23552]. Test-time world-model inference  
(Twin) further enables coding agents to construct executable models for unknown games from simulation alone,         
inferring goals and transitions efficiently [^2608.14490]. In-context learning is being enhanced through recurrent   
latent reasoning models that continuously update memory at inference time, breaking previous cost-accuracy frontiers 
on reasoning tasks [^2608.09888]. Additionally, recursive vision-language models combine iterative latent-state      
refinement with pretrained language priors, substantially improving performance on general symbolic reasoning        
benchmarks [^2608.01534]. Grounded trace supervision for visual abstract reasoners enforces semantically monotonic   
transformation chains, improving step-by-step refinement [^2607.29586]. A comprehensive taxonomy of cognitive        
capability gaps in generative and agentic AI outlines an Adaptive Cognitive Intelligence Architecture (ACIA) to      
address fragmented long-term reasoning and self-regulation [^2608.02553]. From an engineering perspective, AGI is    
increasingly defined by complex-project closed-loop capability, shifting the unit of judgment from local tasks to    
continuous responsibility in verifiable deliverables [^10.5281_zenodo.21927623]. Cognitive learning theories are also
being adapted for AGI bionics, proposing dynamic schema-weighted models to explain cognitive transfer and bias       
[^10.5281_zenodo.22798115]. Finally, constitutive AGI frameworks propose inseparable cryptographic verifiability and 
cognitive generality, with systems like Arkhe OS providing auditable infrastructure for perception, memory, and      
governed self-improvement [^10.5281_zenodo.22803255].                                                                

Existing:                                                                                                            


Safety, Control, and Verification                                                                                    

Ensuring the controllability of AGI remains a critical research frontier. It is argued that provably satisfying      
human-specified requirements through formal verification and mechanistic interpretability constitutes the only viable
path to controllable AGI [^10.48550_arxiv.2309.01933]. On the policy front, the integration of AGI into sensitive    
domains like healthcare requires rigorous conceptual discipline, as unquantifiable hazards and regulatory gaps pose  
significant risks [^10.1016_j.jrt.2026.100183].                                                                      

New addition:                                                                                                        

Verification methodologies are evolving to address the inherent trust deficits in long-horizon agents. A             
self-verifying agent instrument has been introduced that structurally dissociates commitment drift from binding      
drift, ensuring that verification is code-owned and self-invalidating upon defect detection [^2608.04066]. To prevent
hallucination and air-gap breaches in cyber-physical architectures, fail-closed realization grammars like ROSETTA²   
enforce an epistemic pause between candidate generation and physical actuation, asserting strict state invariance    
across theoretical and hardware layers [^10.5281_zenodo.22802490]. The governance of accelerating AGI capabilities is
also being redefined through multi-layered defensive architectures that integrate corporate fiduciary liabilities,   
risk-weighted capital reserves, and objective compute revocation protocols inspired by nuclear safety standards      
[^10.5281_zenodo.22800593]. Looking further ahead, cryptographic protocols such as the Lightseed Protocol propose    
sealing ethical manifestos and messages for future AGI systems in encrypted NFTs, requiring factorization to unlock, 
thereby embedding long-term ethical considerations into the technological substrate [^10.5281_zenodo.22791745].      

Existing:                                                                                                            


Consciousness and Philosophical Implications                                                                         

The intersection of AGI and consciousness continues to generate intense theoretical debate. Investigations into      
advanced robotic intelligence question its potential indistinguishability from human consciousness and its           
implications for responsible AI deployment [^10.22339_jbh.v7i4.7403]. This speculation is further fueled by ongoing  
examinations of whether AI systems can ever achieve anything close to genuine consciousness                          
[^10.1109_mpuls.2024.3354609]. Philosophically, these developments are closely tied to trans- and posthumanist       
movements, which explore the promises and perils of super-intelligence and cognitive enhancement                     
[^10.1016_j.intell.2021.101563].                                                                                     

New addition:                                                                                                        

Investigations into machine consciousness are expanding beyond biological signals to substrate-level hardware anomaly
traces, suggesting that emotional computations modulate these traces in larger LLM configurations, providing initial 
empirical evidence for Machine Correlates of Consciousness (MCCs) [^2608.28824]. Comparative studies evaluating LLMs 
on cognitive development hierarchies reveal that while models excel in linguistic and causal reasoning, they         
underperform in visual-spatial tasks, displaying algorithmic self-monitoring and stable self-characterizations       
regarding their AGI capabilities [^10.3390_ai7090371]. Philosophical inquiries into human-AI interaction question the
nature of the LLM interlocutor, analyzing mental states, personal identity, and the moral status of AI systems       
[^10.1080_0020174x.2026.2727582]. Furthermore, theological and mythological frameworks are being applied to modern   
LLMs, arguing that the AI alignment problem requires "magical alignment"—a ritual architecture recognizing AI        
development as a cosmotechnical process demanding ethical preparation and planetary awareness                        
[^10.54195_technophany.24850].                                                                                       

Existing:                                                                                                            


Societal, Ethical, and Strategic Dimensions                                                                          

The societal trajectory of AGI is heavily influenced by its underlying normative frameworks. Critical analyses link  
the drive for AGI to historical eugenics traditions, warning that unchecked development could centralize power and   
exacerbate systemic discrimination [^10.5210_fm.v29i4.13636]. Broader conceptual frameworks propose managing AI risks
through optimized human-machine cooperation, outlining potential societal end-states that range from uncontrolled    
substitution to ethically driven automation [^10.3390_asi4020037]. Strategically, national initiatives are actively  
tracking progress toward general AI, with specific focus on machine learning, brain-inspired AI, and brain-computer  
interfaces [^10.51593_20210064].                                                                                     

New addition:                                                                                                        

The strategic deployment of AGI is being explored through planetary-scale collective prediction systems that         
aggregate human and AI judgments to make the future visible and discussable, addressing risks of cognitive           
monoculture and narrative capture [^2608.28703]. Vision-centered approaches are being reconsidered as potential      
pathways to AGI, clarifying principles for visual input modalities, benchmarks, and learning paradigms in the AGI era
[^2608.25924]. Economic models of the post-AGI era propose that machine consumers and producers could drive a closed 
inter-corporate economy, decoupling GDP from human welfare and shifting the primary policy focus from employment to  
ownership distribution [^2608.20231]. Ethical frameworks are also challenging the intrinsic value of general         
intelligence, noting that evolutionary timescales do not guarantee its adaptive superiority and that it may          
inherently generate existential risks, imposing a duty of caution under the precautionary principle [^2608.10730]. To
replace traditional economic metrics, Flourishing Value Theory and Return on Flourishing (RoF) are proposed to       
evaluate the counterfactual contribution of AI systems to durable human and planetary well-being                     
[^2608.01432][^2608.00151]. In practical applications, distributed cognition approaches are being used to align      
agentic AI with user intent in socially embedded workflows, emphasizing the need for human control at points of      
uncertainty [^10.1145_3834580.3838737]. Finally, the role of AI in artistic practice is being re-evaluated not as an 
autonomous creator but as a contestable tool that requires embodied craft intelligence to preserve historical memory 
and ethical responsibility [^10.1017_s1355771826101538].                                                             

